Q1. What is Docker Engine?

Interview Ready Answer

Docker Engine is the core runtime of Docker. It is responsible for
building, running, and managing Docker containers and images. Docker
Engine consists of three main components: Docker CLI, Docker Daemon,
and Docker REST API.

Definition

Docker Engine
- Core runtime of Docker.
- Executes Docker operations.
- Manages containers, images, networks, and volumes.

Purpose

• Build Docker images.
• Run Docker containers.
• Manage Docker resources.
• Process Docker commands.

======================================================================

Q2. What is Docker Daemon?

Interview Ready Answer

Docker Daemon (dockerd) is the background service of Docker Engine. It
listens for Docker API requests and performs Docker operations such as
building images, creating containers, starting or stopping containers,
and managing networks and volumes.

Definition

Docker Daemon
- Background service.
- Performs actual Docker operations.

Purpose

• Build images.
• Create containers.
• Start/Stop containers.
• Manage networks.
• Manage volumes.

======================================================================

Q3. What is Docker CLI?

Interview Ready Answer

Docker CLI (Command Line Interface) is the command-line tool used to
interact with Docker. It accepts Docker commands from the user and sends
them to the Docker Daemon through the Docker REST API.

Definition

Docker CLI
- Command-line interface.
- Used by users to execute Docker commands.

Purpose

• Accept user commands.
• Communicate with Docker Daemon.
• Trigger Docker operations.

======================================================================

Q4. What is Docker REST API?

Interview Ready Answer

Docker REST API is an interface that allows the Docker CLI and other
applications to communicate with the Docker Daemon. It forwards Docker
requests to the daemon for execution.

Definition

Docker REST API
- Communication interface.
- Connects Docker CLI with Docker Daemon.

Purpose

• Transfer Docker requests.
• Enable communication between CLI and Daemon.
• Allow external applications to control Docker.

